Your working directory is empty, so I'll answer generally — paste the component (parent + child) if you want me to pinpoint it.

## The core reason

`React.memo` (and React's own bail-out) compare props with `Object.is`. "Same props" to you usually means *structurally equal*, but React sees *referentially different*. Every parent render creates new object/array/function literals:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <Child
    style={{ color: 'red' }}        // new object every render
    items={data.filter(x => x.ok)}  // new array every render
    onClick={() => doThing()}       // new function every render
  />;
}
```

Even if the child is wrapped in `memo`, all three props fail the identity check, so it re-renders.

## Also check these

1. **The child isn't memoized at all.** Without `React.memo`, a child re-renders whenever its parent does — regardless of props. That's normal React behavior, not a bug. `memo` is opt-in.

2. **Context.** If the child calls `useContext`, it re-renders when the provider's value changes, and `memo` does not stop that. A provider whose `value={{ user, setUser }}` is an inline object changes identity on every parent render.

3. **`children` as a prop.** `<Memoized>{<Thing/>}</Memoized>` creates a new element object each render, so `children` always differs.

## Fixes

```jsx
const Child = memo(function Child({ style, items, onClick }) { /* ... */ });

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red' }), []);
  const items = useMemo(() => data.filter(x => x.ok), [data]);
  const onClick = useCallback(() => doThing(), []);
  return <Child style={style} items={items} onClick={onClick} />;
}
```

For context, memoize the provider value: `const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);`

## How to confirm which one it is

React DevTools → Profiler → gear icon → "Record why each component rendered." It will tell you literally *"Props changed: (style)"* or *"Context changed"* or *"The parent component rendered."* That last message means you're in case #1 — no `memo`.

One caveat worth stating: if the child is cheap to render, none of this is worth doing. `memo`/`useMemo`/`useCallback` have their own cost, and a re-render that produces the same output is just a diff against an identical tree. Only chase this if the Profiler shows real time being spent.
